   invisible, so excluding them keeps the fused effect observable.\n-#    - Placeholder colors are capped at 2-4 and each chosen color is used at\n-#      least twice inside the frame.  That keeps the scene readable and repeats\n-#      the same transformed hollow template multiple times.\n-#    - Every generated sprite must satisfy three checks simultaneously:\n-#      it has at least one enclosed hole, outlining removes at least one pixel,\n-#      and the outlined result is still non-empty.  This prevents silent\n-#      regression to plain D3 or plain D5 behavior.\n-#\n-# c) Old assumptions that must change:\n-#    - The original solver effectively assumed an outside sprite mask could be\n-#      written back directly.  That is no longer valid; the solver must rebuild\n-#      a mask, outline it, and only then stamp it.\n-#    - Hollow sprites introduce bg inside the bbox, but this does not break\n-#      placeholder detection because placeholders inside the frame remain solid\n-#      NxN blocks.\n-#    - Legend parsing still relies on compact two-color overlapping glyphs with\n-#      no bg in their union bbox.  Because sprites and legends are placed with a\n-#      full-bbox reserve mask, hollow sprite holes cannot leak into legend bboxes.\n-#    - The \"one size-matched sprite per placeholder color\" assumption still\n-#      holds because the generator keeps exactly one external template bbox for\n-#      each source color and keeps legends at fixed 3x4 size.\n-#\n-# d) Placement legality:\n-#    - Inside the frame, placeholders still live on an NxN grid with one-cell bg\n-#      gaps, so even outlined hollow sprites never overlap after stamping.\n-#    - Outside the frame, both legends and sprites reserve their full bboxes plus\n-#      padding, so no two patches overlap and no legend can sit inside a sprite\n-#      hole.\n-#    - The generator is output-first: it constructs the expected outlined-hollow\n-#      output frame, backfills the input placeholders and external cues, then\n-#      runs `main()` as an oracle.\n-#\n-# e) Visual observability:\n-#    - A human can see the hollow-source rule directly because every outside\n-#      sprite has an enclosed bg hole.  A human can see the outline-placement\n-#      rule because the frame output is thinner than the external template while\n-#      preserving the inner-hole boundary.\n-#    - No ordering depends on numeric color ids.  Colors only serve as visible\n-#      equality keys: placeholder color selects the source sprite, and legend\n-#      pairing selects the final recolor.\n-#\n-# f) Visual cleanliness:\n-#    - Sprites stay small and thick enough to show nested contours cleanly.\n-#      Reducing the number of placeholder colors avoids a noisy legend field.\n-#    - Targets are recolored uniformly per source color, and the frame border plus\n-#      bg gaps keep the transformed regions visually separated.\n-#\n-# g) Change inferability:\n-#    - The recoloring rule remains structurally explained by the same outside\n-#      legends as the original task, so the only new inference burden is local:\n-#      \"copy the hollow sprite, but keep only its contour pixels.\"\n-#    - Because each grid repeats source colors multiple times, the solver gets\n-#      repeated evidence that the same hollow template is reused consistently.\n-#\n-# Post-change solver assumptions reviewed against the generator:\n-#    - Every placeholder color has exactly one non-legend sprite bbox of the same\n-#      size: guaranteed.\n-#    - Every external sprite has >=1 enclosed hole: guaranteed by `_hole_count`.\n-#    - Every external sprite loses pixels under outlining: guaranteed by\n-#      `_outline_mask(mask).sum() < mask.sum()`.\n-#    - Outlined masks stay non-empty and fit exactly inside placeholder bboxes:\n-#      guaranteed.\n-#    - Legends use target colors outside the placeholder-color set, so legend\n-#      source/target inference remains unambiguous.\n+# Design analysis for the D3/1 + D5/3 combo variant\n+# --------------------------------------------------\n+# variant:\n+#   core_change:\n+#     原题把框外同色模板完整搬进框内，再按图例重着色。这里做两层叠加：\n+#     1) 框外模板本身已经是“空心精灵”，即其 bbox 内存在被同色像素完全包围的背景洞。\n+#     2) 搬运时不保留整块模板，而是对该空心模板取 4 邻域轮廓：任何上下左右四个方向\n+#        都还是前景的像素都会被删掉。\n+#     轮廓定义因此不是“只保留最外侧外形”，而是“保留所有与背景接触的前景像素”。\n+#     对空心模板来说，洞边界同样接触背景，所以内环和外环都会被保留，形成双轮廓。\n+#   concrete_behavior:\n+#     一个可视化小例子：若框外 5x5 精灵是\n+#       11111\n+#       11111\n+#       11011\n+#       11111\n+#       11111\n+#     原题会把这 24 个前景像素全贴进框内；只做 D3/1 时仍会得到同一空心块。\n+#     本融合版先承认中心 0 是封闭洞，再做 D5/3 轮廓化，于是外围方框保留、洞周围的\n+#     内边界也保留，而厚壁中间那些四邻都为 1 的像素会被删掉，出现“外环 + 内环”的\n+#     嵌套轮廓。这正是单独任何一项变体都不稳定产生的视觉结果。\n+#   complexity_guardrails:\n+#     - 若模板没有洞，融合会退化成普通轮廓放置。\n+#     - 若模板虽然有洞，但墙厚只有 1，则轮廓化后与原空心模板相同，D5/3 不可观察。\n+#     因此生成器强制每个模板同时满足：\n+#       * bbox 内至少有一个封闭背景洞；\n+#       * 轮廓化时至少会删掉一个像素；\n+#       * 轮廓结果仍同时包含外边界像素与洞边界像素。\n+#     这让 2-4 个样例就能稳定暴露“空心对象 + 嵌套轮廓”规律。\n+#   solver_assumption_audit:\n+#     - `_find_frame` 只依赖唯一矩形�[str, int]:\n-    edges = [\"top\", \"bottom\", \"left\", \"right\"]\n-    used_count = min(4, max(2, k))\n-    used_edges = random.sample(edges, used_count)\n-    counts = {edge: 0 for edge in edges}\n-    for edge in used_edges:\n-        counts[edge] = 1\n-    for _ in range(k - used_count):\n-        counts[random.choice(used_edges)] += 1\n-    return counts\n-\n-\n-def _legend_layout(\n-    src_cols: list[int], n: int, h: int, w: int\n-) -> dict[int, tuple[str, int]] | None:\n-    counts = _choose_edge_counts(len(src_cols))\n-    corner_clear = n + 1\n-    starts = {\n-        \"top\": _slot_starts(w, n, counts[\"top\"], corner_clear),\n-        \"bottom\": _slot_starts(w, n, counts[\"bottom\"], corner_clear),\n-        \"left\": _slot_starts(h, n, counts[\"left\"], corner_clear),\n-        \"right\": _slot_starts(h, n, counts[\"right\"], corner_clear),\n-    }\n-    if any(v is None for v in starts.values()):\n-        return None\n-\n-    order = src_cols[:]\n-    random.shuffle(order)\n-    layout: dict[int, tuple[str, int]] = {}\n-    idx = 0\n-    for edge in (\"top\", \"bottom\", \"left\", \"right\"):\n-        for start in starts[edge]:\n-            layout[order[idx]] = (edge, int(start))\n-            idx += 1\n-    assert idx == len(order)\n-    return layout\n-\n-\n-def _place_free_patch(\n-    grid: np.ndarray, reserve: np.ndarray, patch: np.ndarray, bg: int\n-) -> bool:\n+def _place_patch(grid: np.ndarray, reserve: np.ndarray, patch: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> bool:\n     blocker = np.full(patch.shape, FRAME_COL, dtype=int)\n     try:\n-        r, c = random_free_location(reserve, blocker, transparent=bg, padding=PATCH_PADDING, border=1)\n+        r, c = random_free_location(reserve, blocker, transparent=bg, padding=PATCH_PADDING)\n     except ValueError:\n         return False\n     paste(reserve, blocker, r, c)\n     paste(grid, patch, r, c, transparent=bg)\n     return True\n-\n \n-def _collect_placeholders(\n-    crop: np.ndarray, bg: int, frame_col: int\n-) -> tuple[list[tuple[int, tuple[int, int, int, int]]], dict[int, tuple[int, int]], set[int]]:\n-    h, w = crop.shape\n-    inner_mask = np.zeros((h, w), dtype=bool)\n-    inner_mask[1:-1, 1:-1] = True\n-    placeholder_colors = set(map(int, np.unique(crop[inner_mask]))) - {bg, frame_col}\n-    assert placeholder_colors, \"no placeholder colors found inside frame\"\n \n-    placeholders: list[tuple[int, tuple[int, int, int, int]]] = []\n-    for col in sorted(placeholder_colors):\n-        for pts in components_mask((crop == col) & inner_mask, connectivity=4):\n-            r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\n-            placeholders.append((col, (r0, c0, r1, c1)))\n-\n-    phw: dict[int, tuple[int, int]] = {}\n-    for col in placeholder_colors:\n-        dims = [(b[2] - b[0] + 1, b[3] - b[1] + 1) for c, b in placeholders if c == col]\n-        assert dims, f\"no placeholder component found for color {col}\"\n-        unique_dims = sorted(set(dims))\n-        assert len(unique_dims) == 1, f\"ambiguous placeholder sizes for color {col}: {unique_dims}\"\n-        phw[col] = unique_dims[0]\n-    return placeholders, phw, placeholder_colors\n-\n-\n-def _touches_boundary(bbox: tuple[int, int, int, int], shape: tuple[int, int]) -> bool:\n-    r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox\n-    h, w = shape\n-    return r0 == 0 or c0 == 0 or r1 == h - 1 or c1 == w - 1\n-\n-\n-def _extract_boundary_legend_tiles(\n-    g: np.ndarray,\n-    outside: np.ndarray,\n-    bg: int,\n-    frame_col: int,\n-    valid_sizes: set[tuple[int, int]],\n-) -> tuple[list[dict], np.ndarray]:\n-    legend_tiles = []\n-    legend_mask = np.zeros_like(g, dtype=bool)\n-\n-    for pts in components_mask((g != bg) & outside, connectivity=4):\n-        r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\n-        hh, ww = r1 - r0 + 1, c1 - c0 + 1\n-        if (hh, ww) not in valid_sizes:\n-            continue\n-        if len(pts) != hh * ww:\n-            continue\n-        if not _touches_boundary((r0, c0, r1, c1), g.shape):\n-            continue\n-        sub = g[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]\n-        sub_colors = set(map(int, np.unique(sub)))\n-        if bg in sub_colors or frame_col in sub_colors or len(sub_colors) != 2:\n-            continue\n-        legend_tiles.append({\"bbox\": (r0, c0, r1, c1), \"sub\": sub.copy(), \"colors\": sub_colors})\n-        legend_mask[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1] = True\n-\n-    assert legend_tiles, \"no boundary legend tiles found\"\n-    shared = set.intersection(*(set(tile[\"colors\"]) for tile in legend_tiles))\n-    assert len(shared) == 1, f\"expected exactly one common legend ink color, found {sorted(shared)}\"\n-    legend_ink = int(next(iter(shared)))\n-\n-    legend_entries = []\n-    for tile in legend_tiles:\n-        others = sorted(tile[\"colors\"] - {legend_ink})\n-        assert len(others) == 1, \"legend tile must contain one target color besides the common ink\"\n-        shape_mask = tile[\"sub\"] == legend_ink\n-        assert np.any(shape_mask) and np.any(~shape_mask), \"legend tile must show both shape and background\"\n-        legend_entries.append(\n-            {\n-                \"shape_mask\": shape_mask.copy(),\n-                \"tgt\": int(others[0]),\n-                \"bbox\": tile[\"bbox\"],\n-            }\n-        )\n-    return legend_entries, legend_mask\n-\n-\n-def _extract_sprite_masks(\n-    g: np.ndarray,\n-    outside: np.ndarray,\n-    legend_mask: np.ndarray,\n-    placeholder_colors: set[int],\n-    phw: dict[int, tuple[int, int]],\n-) -> dict[int, np.ndarray]:\n-    sprite_masks: dict[int, np.ndarray] = {}\n-    work = outside & (~legend_mask)\n-    h, w = g.shape\n-    work[0, :] = False\n-    work[-1, :] = False\n-    work[:, 0] = False\n-    work[:, -1] = False\n-\n-    for src_col in sorted(placeholder_colors):\n-        comps = []\n-        for pts in components_mask((g == src_col) & work, connectivity=4):\n-            r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(pts)\n-            comps.append({\"pts\": [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in pts], \"bbox\": (r0, c0, r1, c1)})\n-        assert comps, f\"no source sprite found for color {src_col}\"\n-        want_h, want_w = phw[src_col]\n-        sized = [c for c in comps if (c[\"bbox\"][2] - c[\"bbox\"][0] + 1, c[\"bbox\"][3] - c[\"bbox\"][1] + 1) == (want_h, want_w)]\n-        assert len(sized) == 1, f\"expected one size-matched sprite for color {src_col}, found {len(sized)}\"\n-        base = sized[0]\n-        sr0, sc0, sr1, sc1 = base[\"bbox\"]\n-        assert (sr1 - sr0 + 1) == (sc1 - sc0 + 1), \"quadrant rotation requires square sprite boxes\"\n-\n-        pts_set = set(base[\"pts\"])\n-        for comp in comps:\n-            if comp is base:\n-                continue\n-            r0, c0, r1, c1 = comp[\"bbox\"]\n-            assert sr0 <= r0 and sr1 >= r1 and sc0 <= c0 and sc1 >= c1, (\n-                f\"extra sprite component for color {src_col} is outside the base bbox\"\n-            )\n-            pts_set.update(comp[\"pts\"])\n-\n-        mask = np.zeros((sr1 - sr0 + 1, sc1 - sc0 + 1), dtype=bool)\n-        for r, c in pts_set:\n-            mask[int(r - sr0), int(c - sc0)] = True\n-        sprite_masks[src_col] = mask\n-    return sprite_masks\n-\n-\n-def _extract_instance(grid: np.ndarray) -> dict:\n-    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n-    bg = get_bg(g)\n-    frame_col, (fr0, fc0, fr1, fc1) = _find_frame(g, bg)\n-\n-    crop = g[fr0 : fr1 + 1, fc0 : fc1 + 1]\n-    placeholders, phw, placeholder_colors = _collect_placeholders(crop, bg, frame_col)\n-\n-    outside = np.ones(g.shape, dtype=bool)\n-    outside[fr0 : fr1 + 1, fc0 : fc1 + 1] = False\n-\n-    legend_entries, legend_mask = _extract_boundary_legend_tiles(g, outside, bg, frame_col, set(phw.values()))\n-    assert len(legend_entries) == len(placeholder_colors), \"legend tile count must equal number of source colors\"\n-\n-    sprite_masks = _extract_sprite_masks(g, outside, legend_mask, placeholder_colors, phw)\n-    assert set(sprite_masks) == placeholder_colors\n-\n-    return {\n-        \"grid\": g,\n-        \"bg\": int(bg),\n-        \"frame_col\": int(frame_col),\n-        \"frame_bbox\": (fr0, fc0, fr1, fc1),\n-        \"crop\": crop,\ginal recolor map was `src_color -> tgt_color`. That becomes\n-#      `src_color -> sprite_mask -> dihedral-matched legend tile -> tgt_color`.\n-#    - Original paste step assumed sprite orientation is preserved. That fails because placement now depends on\n-#      frame quadrant, so `main()` must rotate after source-sprite extraction and after legend matching.\n-#    - Original outside-object search could treat every non-frame component equally. Here `main()` must first\n-#      exclude boundary-touching full rectangles as legend tiles so source sprite extraction is unambiguous.\n-#    - New assumptions introduced here and enforced by the generator:\n-#      every legend tile is a full square touching the boundary; non-legend sprites never touch the boundary;\n-#      placeholder boxes are square and never lie on the frame midlines; sprite masks are unique up to dihedral\n-#      symmetry.\n-# d) Placement legality\n-#    - The frame is placed away from the outer boundary by an edge band of at least N cells, so edge legends\n-#      never overlap the frame.\n-#    - Legend tiles on the same edge have at least one background gap, and corner clearance prevents top/bottom\n-#      tiles from colliding with left/right tiles.\n-#    - Source sprites are placed only in unreserved space using `random_free_location`, so they do not overlap\n-#      the frame, legends, or each other.\n-# e) Visual observability\n-#    - A human sees: boundary legend tiles in odd orientations, source sprites floating outside the frame, and\n-#      repeated placeholder colors appearing with different final orientations across quadrants.\n-#    - From a few examples one can infer:\n-#      color chooses source sprite, shape-under-transform chooses target color, position chooses final rotation.\n-#      No rule depends on invisible ordering or raw color ids.\n-# f) Visual cleanliness\n-#    - The frame stays central and uncluttered.\n-#    - Legends occupy the boundary, sprites stay in the middle outside region, and each region has one role.\n-#    - Using intact edge tiles keeps the legend readable even though it is dispersed.\n-# g) Color-change inferability\n-#    - There are only 2-5 source colors and the legend explicitly pairs each transformed shape with a target\n-#      background color.\n-#    - Because the same source color appears in multiple frame positions, the user can separate \"which color\n-#      does this shape become?\" from \"which way does it rotate when pasted?\"\n+#      suppose color 3 selects source sprite S. A placeholder of color 3 in the lower-left quadrant uses rot270(S)\n+#      when pasted into the frame. On the canvas edge, its legend fragment may display flip(rot90(S)).\n+#      The solver must reconstruct the split edge legend, match S to that displayed silhouette up to transform,\n+#      obtain the target color, and then recolor the already-rotated placement.\n+#      The two rotations live in different stages: one controls placement orientation, one controls legend reading.\n+#\n+# b) Complexity calibration:\n+#    - To keep the two rotation sources visible, every sprite mask is generated with four distinct 90-degree rotations.\n+#    - To keep D2 unambiguous, masks are unique up to full dihedral signature, so one legend silhouette matches one sprite only.\n+#    - To keep D5 visible, filled placeholders cover all four quadrants and at least one source color repeats across\n+#      different quadrants, producing same-shape/different-orientation outputs.\n+#    - To keep D7 visible, every sample uses both vertical (top+bottom) and horizontal (left+right) split entries.\n+#\n+# c) Implicit-assumption audit:\n+#    - Original legend parsing assumed overlapping local blobs. That is invalid: legend data is now spread over the\n+#      outer boundary, so the solver must parse boundary runs and dedicated edge bands instead.\n+#    - Original recolor lookup used source color directly. That also breaks: the edge legend carries no source color\n+#      identity, only shape identity in a transformed orientation, so the solver must extract sprite masks first and\n+#      then match them to legend silhouettes up to rotation/flip.\n+#    - Original placement copied the sprite mask as-is. Now quadrant-based rotation happens before pasting.\n+#    - New combo-specific assumption: all placeholder bboxes share one square NxN size. D5 needs square rotation and\n+#      the D7 edge fragments use the same N as their span. The generator enforces this globally.\n+#    - Another new assumption: no free sprite touches the grid boundary. The generator reserves the whole boundary so\n+#      every boundary-touching object belongs to the split legend.\n+#\n+# d) Placement legality:\n+#    - The frame is placed away from the outer boundary by at least one legend band.\n+#    - The full boundary is reserved before random sprite placement, so sprites cannot overlap legends or touch edges.\n+#    - Top/left source fragments are kept away from corners by `corner_clear = n + 2`, preventing cross-edge overlap.\n+#    - Rotated placements stay inside their original NxN placeholder bboxes because all sprites are square.\n+#\n+# e) Visual observability:\n+#    - Humans can see two distinct regularities:\n+#      1. identical source colors yield the same base shape but different in-frame orientations by quadrant;\n+#      2. edge fragments must be paired across opposite sides, and the displayed legend silhouette often differs from\n+#         the source sprite's raw orientation.\n+#    - Neither rule depends on numeric color ids or hidden ordering.\n+#\n+# f) Visual cleanliness:\n+#    - The central frame remains visually separate from the edge legends.\n+#    - The split legends use thin boundary-aligned structures, leaving the remaining outside area for free sprites.\n+#    - Each region carries one role only: frame/interior, edge legend, or free sprites.\n+#\n+# g) Color inferability and generator guarantees:\n+#    - Target colors are still given explicitly by opposite-edge bars, so recoloring is structured rather than arbitrary.\n+#    - The generator guarantees:\n+#      * every legend silhouette is a non-identity dihedral transform of its sprite;\n+#      * all four quadrant rotations appear among filled placeholders;\n+#      * at least one source color repeats across different quadrants;\n+#      * both vertical and horizontal split legend entries are present;\n+#      * sprite masks are unique up to dihedral symmetry and visibly change under rotation.\n \n \n FRAME_COL = 5\n@@ -90,17 +90,17 @@\n MAX_EMPTY_SLOTS = 2\n NUM_COLORS_RANGE = (2, 5)\n SPRITE_FILL_MIN = 0.45\n-SPRITE_FILL_MAX = 0.82\n+SPRITE_FILL_MAX = 0.8\n MIN_FRAME_H = 13\n MIN_FRAME_W = 18\n MAX_